1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,244
Right now, your head is older
than your feet.

2
00:00:04,733 --> 00:00:05,668
Not by much.

3
00:00:05,718 --> 00:00:12,482
Over an entire lifetime, the difference adds up
to less than a tenth of a millionth

4
00:00:12,532 --> 00:00:13,467
of a second.

5
00:00:13,967 --> 00:00:16,148
But it's real. It has been measured.

6
00:00:16,198 --> 00:00:21,166
And the experiments that measured it are some
of the strangest things humans have

7
00:00:21,216 --> 00:00:21,786
ever done.

8
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:26,887
We raced particles down a mountain. We
bought plane tickets for clocks.

9
00:00:26,937 --> 00:00:32,242
We lifted a clock by the height of two stair
steps… and then we did something even

10
00:00:32,292 --> 00:00:33,026
more absurd.

11
00:00:33,533 --> 00:00:37,202
Four experiments. Each one
smaller than the last.

12
00:00:37,252 --> 00:00:41,679
And by the end, time won't be the
thing you thought it was.

13
00:00:44,766 --> 00:00:49,518
Every second, particles from deep space slam
into the top of our atmosphere.

14
00:00:49,568 --> 00:00:54,573
The collisions set off showers of debris — and
some of that debris is a particle

15
00:00:54,623 --> 00:00:55,584
called the muon.

16
00:00:56,099 --> 00:00:59,686
A muon is like a heavier cousin
of the electron,

17
00:00:59,736 --> 00:01:02,565
with one big problem: it doesn't last.

18
00:01:02,615 --> 00:01:07,641
On average, a muon falls apart in about
two millionths of a second.

19
00:01:08,133 --> 00:01:12,141
And muons are born high up — many
kilometers above our heads.

20
00:01:12,191 --> 00:01:14,868
Even moving at nearly the speed of light,

21
00:01:14,918 --> 00:01:18,859
most of them should decay long before
they reach the ground.

22
00:01:19,366 --> 00:01:23,390
And yet, detectors at sea level
catch them all the time.

23
00:01:23,899 --> 00:01:28,536
In the early 1960s, two physicists,
David Frisch and James Smith,

24
00:01:28,586 --> 00:01:30,771
decided to count them properly.

25
00:01:30,821 --> 00:01:36,683
They hauled a detector to the summit of Mount
Washington in New Hampshire — almost

26
00:01:36,733 --> 00:01:41,514
two kilometers up — and counted muons
moving at one specific speed.

27
00:01:41,999 --> 00:01:46,963
Then they brought the detector down to sea
level, in Cambridge, Massachusetts,

28
00:01:47,013 --> 00:01:48,120
and counted again.

29
00:01:48,633 --> 00:01:50,303
The math looked simple.

30
00:01:50,353 --> 00:01:55,910
Given how long a muon lives, and how long
the trip down the mountain takes,

31
00:01:55,960 --> 00:01:58,602
then for every 568 muons at the top,

32
00:01:58,652 --> 00:02:02,265
only about 27 should have
survived to the bottom.

33
00:02:02,766 --> 00:02:04,512
They counted 412.

34
00:02:04,999 --> 00:02:07,725
Fifteen times more than expected.

35
00:02:08,233 --> 00:02:11,365
The only explanation that
fits is Einstein's.

36
00:02:11,415 --> 00:02:16,810
For a particle moving at more than
ninety-nine percent of the speed of light,

37
00:02:16,860 --> 00:02:22,750
time runs slow. From our point of view, the
muon's internal clock is barely ticking.

38
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:28,265
A trip that should take several of its
lifetimes takes only a fraction of one.

39
00:02:28,766 --> 00:02:30,865
The muons weren't breaking the rules.

40
00:02:30,915 --> 00:02:33,712
They were living in a different
time than we are.

41
00:02:34,199 --> 00:02:40,730
But muons are exotic — tiny, fast, far away.
What about something ordinary?

42
00:02:40,780 --> 00:02:42,924
Like a clock… on a plane.

43
00:02:46,033 --> 00:02:51,629
In October 1971, a physicist named Joseph
Hafele and an astronomer from the U.S.

44
00:02:51,679 --> 00:02:56,851
Naval Observatory, Richard Keating, did
something that sounds like a joke.

45
00:02:57,366 --> 00:03:00,092
They bought plane tickets for
atomic clocks.

46
00:03:00,599 --> 00:03:06,810
Four cesium-beam clocks — among the most
precise timekeepers of the era — flew on

47
00:03:06,860 --> 00:03:12,840
ordinary commercial flights, all the way
around the world. First heading east.

48
00:03:12,890 --> 00:03:14,231
Then heading west.

49
00:03:14,733 --> 00:03:19,596
Back in Washington, the Naval Observatory's
clocks stayed on the ground,

50
00:03:19,646 --> 00:03:20,756
ticking as usual.

51
00:03:21,266 --> 00:03:26,289
Einstein's theories predicted two effects
pulling in opposite directions.

52
00:03:26,339 --> 00:03:27,818
Speed slows time down.

53
00:03:27,868 --> 00:03:32,751
Height speeds it up — the weaker the
gravity, the faster a clock ticks.

54
00:03:32,801 --> 00:03:35,601
And because the Earth itself is spinning,

55
00:03:35,651 --> 00:03:40,187
flying east or west changes how fast the
clocks are really moving.

56
00:03:40,699 --> 00:03:45,645
When the clocks came home, they no longer agreed
with the ones that stayed behind.

57
00:03:46,099 --> 00:03:51,402
The eastbound clocks had lost about 59
billionths of a second.

58
00:03:51,452 --> 00:03:55,029
The westbound clocks had gained about 273.

59
00:03:55,533 --> 00:03:56,784
The predictions?

60
00:03:56,834 --> 00:04:03,536
A loss of about 40, and a gain of about 275 —
agreement within the uncertainties of

61
00:04:03,586 --> 00:04:04,756
the experiment.

62
00:04:05,266 --> 00:04:10,139
Two trips around the planet. A few
hundred billionths of a second.

63
00:04:10,189 --> 00:04:12,302
And Einstein was right again.

64
00:04:12,799 --> 00:04:15,227
Today, this isn't just a curiosity.

65
00:04:15,277 --> 00:04:19,262
GPS satellites orbit about twenty
thousand kilometers up,

66
00:04:19,312 --> 00:04:25,066
and their clocks run faster than ours by about
thirty-eight millionths of a second

67
00:04:25,116 --> 00:04:25,774
every day.

68
00:04:25,824 --> 00:04:31,366
Leave that uncorrected, and your phone's map
would drift by kilometers within a

69
00:04:31,416 --> 00:04:32,145
single day.

70
00:04:32,633 --> 00:04:35,670
But a plane still flies ten
kilometers high.

71
00:04:35,720 --> 00:04:39,669
How small can the difference get —
and still be measured?

72
00:04:42,766 --> 00:04:48,187
In 2010, physicists at the National
Institute of Standards and Technology,

73
00:04:48,237 --> 00:04:53,584
in Boulder, Colorado, had built something
far better than a cesium clock.

74
00:04:54,099 --> 00:05:00,353
Their clocks ticked using a single aluminum ion
— one electrically charged atom,

75
00:05:00,403 --> 00:05:04,923
held in place by electric fields and
read out with lasers.

76
00:05:04,973 --> 00:05:10,833
The best of them kept time to within one
second in about 3.7 billion years.

77
00:05:10,399 --> 00:05:14,920
So they took two of these clocks, and
asked a very small question.

78
00:05:15,433 --> 00:05:19,780
They raised one of them by 33
centimeters. About a foot.

79
00:05:19,830 --> 00:05:22,371
Roughly two steps on a staircase.

80
00:05:22,866 --> 00:05:25,004
And the higher clock ran faster.

81
00:05:25,499 --> 00:05:26,486
Not by much.

82
00:05:26,536 --> 00:05:33,743
Over a 79-year lifetime, that difference adds up
to about 90 billionths of a second.

83
00:05:34,233 --> 00:05:39,443
But it's measurable. Every time you climb the
stairs, you age a tiny bit faster.

84
00:05:39,493 --> 00:05:43,848
Every time you lie down, your head and
your feet fall back in step.

85
00:05:44,333 --> 00:05:47,732
They tested the other half of
Einstein's idea too.

86
00:05:47,782 --> 00:05:53,388
They set one ion moving back and forth at just a
few meters per second — about the

87
00:05:53,438 --> 00:05:56,561
speed of a jog — and that
clock ticked slower.

88
00:05:57,066 --> 00:06:03,089
Time bends with height. Time bends with
speed. Even at human scale.

89
00:06:03,599 --> 00:06:06,618
But thirty-three centimeters
still wasn't the limit.

90
00:06:09,733 --> 00:06:16,701
In 2022, a team led by physicist Jun Ye at JILA
— a joint institute of NIST and the

91
00:06:16,751 --> 00:06:22,450
University of Colorado Boulder — pushed
the question to its extreme.

92
00:06:22,199 --> 00:06:26,133
They didn't compare two clocks.
They used one cloud.

93
00:06:26,633 --> 00:06:29,354
About a hundred thousand strontium atoms,

94
00:06:29,404 --> 00:06:34,424
trapped in a vacuum chamber and cooled to a
tiny fraction of a degree above

95
00:06:34,474 --> 00:06:35,370
absolute zero.

96
00:06:35,420 --> 00:06:40,980
Laser light stacks them into thin layers — like
a stack of pancakes made of light —

97
00:06:41,030 --> 00:06:43,954
and every atom in the stack
becomes a clock.

98
00:06:44,466 --> 00:06:50,098
The whole stack is about a millimeter tall.
Roughly the width of a sharp pencil tip.

99
00:06:50,599 --> 00:06:54,565
Then they compared the ticking at the top of
the cloud with the ticking at the

100
00:06:54,615 --> 00:06:54,925
bottom.

101
00:06:55,433 --> 00:06:57,767
And the top was ticking faster.

102
00:06:58,266 --> 00:07:03,450
Across one millimeter, the difference in rate
is about one part in ten billion

103
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:03,987
billion.

104
00:07:04,037 --> 00:07:09,557
To see it, the team compared frequencies with an
uncertainty of just 7.6 parts in a

105
00:07:09,607 --> 00:07:11,235
thousand billion billion.

106
00:07:11,285 --> 00:07:16,805
If a clock were that precise, it would drift
less than a hundredth of a second over

107
00:07:16,855 --> 00:07:18,885
the entire age of the universe.

108
00:07:19,399 --> 00:07:22,405
And they didn't just catch
the effect once.

109
00:07:22,455 --> 00:07:28,233
They saw it as a smooth gradient — tick rates
changing steadily from the bottom of

110
00:07:28,283 --> 00:07:33,422
the cloud to the top — exactly as
Einstein's general relativity predicts.

111
00:07:33,933 --> 00:07:38,203
Our best clocks are now so sensitive that
they can feel time change across

112
00:07:38,253 --> 00:07:40,479
something smaller than a grain of sand.

113
00:07:40,966 --> 00:07:43,887
So here is where four
experiments leave us.

114
00:07:44,399 --> 00:07:49,140
There is no single time. No master clock
ticking for the whole universe.

115
00:07:49,190 --> 00:07:53,864
Every place has its own time — a little
faster where gravity is weaker,

116
00:07:53,914 --> 00:07:56,725
a little slower where you're
moving faster.

117
00:07:57,233 --> 00:08:02,901
A particle racing down a mountain. Clocks with
plane tickets. Two stair steps.

118
00:08:02,951 --> 00:08:04,954
A cloud one millimeter tall.

119
00:08:05,466 --> 00:08:08,208
Time isn't the background of the universe.

120
00:08:08,258 --> 00:08:11,000
It's part of the landscape — and it bends.

121
00:08:11,499 --> 00:08:16,985
And right now, as you watch this, the top of
your head is getting older than your

122
00:08:17,035 --> 00:08:17,327
feet.

123
00:08:17,832 --> 00:08:20,264
Not by much. But it is.
